    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 3.1 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-maxima-and-minima-in-context">
        <title>Maxima and Minima in Context</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> the steps for solving a maximum or minimum problem, and the domain of a model.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> build a one-variable model from a constraint and find its greatest or least value.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> find best dimensions, best prices and least costs.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Groups build open boxes from 24 cm squares of manila paper with different corner cuts, find the best by experiment, then confirm it by calculus.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Give each group a 24 cm square of manila paper, a ruler and scissors, and a different cut <m>x</m> from <m>1</m> to <m>6\,\text{cm}</m>. Before any group measures, ask each to predict which cut will win and write the prediction down. Collect all the volumes on the board before any differentiation, and let each group hear the others' results.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-the-best-box">
            <title>The Best Box</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Squares of side <m>x</m> cm are cut from the four corners of a square sheet of manila paper <m>24\,\text{cm}</m> by <m>24\,\text{cm}</m>, and the sides are folded up to make an open box (<xref ref="fig-open-box-net"/>).</p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Build your group's box. Measure it and work out its volume.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>For <m>x = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6</m>: <m>V = 484, 800, 972, 1024, 980, 864\,\text{cm}^3</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Which cut gives the largest volume so far?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>x = 4</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Show that <m>V = x(24 - 2x)^2 = 4x^3 - 96x^2 + 576x</m>. Why must <m>0 \lt x \lt 12</m>?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>The base is <m>(24 - 2x)</m> cm square and the height is <m>x</m> cm. The cut must be positive, and two cuts must fit along an edge of <m>24\,\text{cm}</m>, so <m>x \lt 12</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>\dfrac{dV}{dx} = 12(x - 4)(x - 12)</m>. Which solution of <m>\dfrac{dV}{dx} = 0</m> gives a box?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>x = 4</m>. At <m>x = 12</m> the base has side <m>0</m>: there is no box.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Use <m>\dfrac{d^2V}{dx^2}</m> to show that <m>x = 4</m> gives the greatest volume. Give the dimensions and volume of the best box.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>\dfrac{d^2V}{dx^2} = 24x - 192 = -96 \lt 0</m> at <m>x = 4</m>. The best box is <m>16\,\text{cm}</m> by <m>16\,\text{cm}</m> by <m>4\,\text{cm}</m>, with volume <m>1024\,\text{cm}^3</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <figure xml:id="fig-open-box-net">
            <caption>The 24 cm square with a square of side <m>x</m> cut from each corner. Fold along the dashed lines.</caption>
            <image width="55%">
                <shortdescription>A 24 cm square with an x by x square cut from each corner and dashed fold lines; the base is 24 minus 2x on each side.</shortdescription>
                <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.22]
\draw[thick] (4,0) -- (20,0) -- (20,4) -- (24,4) -- (24,20) -- (20,20) -- (20,24) -- (4,24) -- (4,20) -- (0,20) -- (0,4) -- (4,4) -- cycle;
\draw[dashed] (4,4) -- (20,4) -- (20,20) -- (4,20) -- cycle;
\draw[gray!60] (0,0) rectangle (4,4); \draw[gray!60] (20,0) rectangle (24,4); \draw[gray!60] (0,20) rectangle (4,24); \draw[gray!60] (20,20) rectangle (24,24);
\node at (2,2) {$x$}; \node at (22,22) {$x$};
\node at (12,12) {base};
\draw[&lt;-&gt;] (4,-1.5) -- (20,-1.5) node[midway, below] {$24 - 2x$};
\draw[&lt;-&gt;] (-1.5,0) -- (-1.5,24) node[midway, left] {$24$};
\end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To set out the method for a maximum or minimum problem.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> the activity follows the steps below in order; compare the experimental winner with the calculus answer.</p></li>
                    <li><p>A solution of <m>\dfrac{dV}{dx} = 0</m> can lie outside the domain: check every solution against the situation.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-solving-a-max-min-problem">
            <title>Solving a Maximum or Minimum Problem</title>
            <p>The best value of a quantity is at a stationary point or at an end of its domain.
                <ol>
                    <li><p>Name the variable and draw a diagram.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Write the quantity in terms of that one variable, using the constraint.</p></li>
                    <li><p>State the domain: the values of the variable that make sense.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Differentiate and solve <m>\text{derivative} = 0</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Confirm whether it is a maximum or a minimum (by the second derivative or the gradient either side).</p></li>
                    <li><p>On a closed domain, compare with the values at the ends.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Answer the question in context, with units.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </insight>

        <figure xml:id="fig-open-box-volume">
            <caption>Drag <m>x</m>. The box folds up and its volume is plotted. Where is <m>V</m> greatest?</caption>
            <interactive xml:id="interactive-open-box-volume" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="16:10" source="jsxgraph/open-box-volume.js">
                <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-open-box-volume" surface="jsxboard" aspect="16:10"/>
                <instructions>
                    <p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><p>Drag the <alert>x</alert> slider. The corner squares grow on the sheet, the box changes shape, and the point on the graph shows its volume.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Set <alert>x</alert> to the cut your group used in <xref ref="act-the-best-box"/> and check your volume.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Find the value of <alert>x</alert> that makes <m>V</m> greatest. Does it agree with <m>x = 4</m>?</p></li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </instructions>
            </interactive>
        </figure>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-chicken-run-against-a-wall">
            <title>A Chicken Run Against a Wall</title>
            <statement><p>Wambui has <m>40\,\text{m}</m> of wire fencing to make a rectangular chicken run against the wall of her house in Nyeri. The wall forms one side. Find the dimensions that give the greatest area.</p></statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Let each side that meets the wall be <m>x</m> m (<xref ref="fig-chicken-run"/>). The fence makes the other three sides, so the side opposite the wall is <m>(40 - 2x)</m> m and
                    <md>A = x(40 - 2x) = 40x - 2x^2, \qquad 0 \lt x \lt 20</md></p>
                <p><m>\dfrac{dA}{dx} = 40 - 4x = 0</m> when <m>x = 10</m>. <m>\dfrac{d^2A}{dx^2} = -4 \lt 0</m>, so this is a maximum.</p>
                <p>The run is <m>10\,\text{m}</m> by <m>20\,\text{m}</m> (the <m>20\,\text{m}</m> side along the wall), with area <m>200\,\text{m}^2</m>.</p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-chicken-run">
                    <caption>A chicken run against a wall.</caption>
                    <image width="55%">
                        <shortdescription>A rectangle against a wall; the two sides meeting the wall are x m and the side opposite the wall is 40 minus 2x m.</shortdescription>
                        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
\fill[pattern=north east lines] (-0.5,3) rectangle (6.5,3.4);
\draw[very thick] (-0.5,3) -- (6.5,3);
\node[above] at (3,3.4) {wall};
\draw[thick] (0,3) -- (0,0) -- (6,0) -- (6,3);
\node[left] at (0,1.5) {$x$}; \node[right] at (6,1.5) {$x$};
\node[below] at (3,0) {$40 - 2x$};
\end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-best-price-for-juice">
            <title>The Best Price for Juice</title>
            <statement><p>Mwanajuma sells cups of sugarcane juice at her stall in Mombasa. Each cup costs her Ksh <m>20</m> to make. At a price of Ksh <m>p</m> a cup she sells <m>300 - 2p</m> cups a day. Find the price that gives the greatest daily profit.</p></statement>
            <solution>
                <p>The profit on each cup is <m>(p - 20)</m> shillings, so the daily profit is
                    <md>P = (p - 20)(300 - 2p) = -2p^2 + 340p - 6000</md></p>
                <p><m>\dfrac{dP}{dp} = -4p + 340 = 0</m> when <m>p = 85</m>, and <m>\dfrac{d^2P}{dp^2} = -4 \lt 0</m>, so this is a maximum.</p>
                <p>At Ksh <m>85</m> a cup she sells <m>130</m> cups, and her profit is <m>65 \times 130 = 8450</m>: Ksh <m>8450</m> a day.</p>
                <p>Notice that her takings alone, <m>p(300 - 2p)</m>, are greatest at Ksh <m>75</m>, a different price. The cost of each cup moves the best price up.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-cheapest-speed-for-a-lorry">
            <title>The Cheapest Speed for a Lorry</title>
            <statement><p>The running cost of a lorry on the Nairobi to Mombasa road, <m>C</m> shillings per km, at a steady speed of <m>v</m> km/h, is modelled by <m>C = 0.004v^2 - 0.64v + 70</m> for <m>40 \le v \le 100</m>. Find the cheapest speed and the cost per km at that speed.</p></statement>
            <solution>
                <p><m>\dfrac{dC}{dv} = 0.008v - 0.64 = 0</m> when <m>v = 80</m>, which is inside the domain. <m>\dfrac{d^2C}{dv^2} = 0.008 \gt 0</m>, so this is a minimum.</p>
                <p><m>C = 0.004 \times 6400 - 0.64 \times 80 + 70 = 25.6 - 51.2 + 70 = 44.4</m>. The cheapest speed is <m>80\,\text{km/h}</m>, costing Ksh <m>44.40</m> per km.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type whole numbers or fractions, for example <c>-3</c> or <c>5/2</c>. Type powers with <c>^</c>, for example <c>2x^3-5x+1</c>.</p>

