        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-best-selling-price-for-the-greatest-profit">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-best-selling-price-for-the-greatest-profit" source="stack/top/4-calculus/17-differentiation-ii/03-application-of-differentiation/Best-selling-price-for-the-greatest-profit.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercises xml:id="exercises-maxima-and-minima-in-context">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-greatest-product">
                <statement><p>Two positive numbers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> have <m>x + y = 12</m>. Find the greatest value of <m>xy^2</m>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>256</m>, when <m>x = 4</m> and <m>y = 8</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>xy^2 = (12 - y)y^2 = 12y^2 - y^3</m>, whose derivative <m>24y - 3y^2 = 3y(8 - y)</m> is <m>0</m> at <m>y = 8</m> (the second derivative <m>24 - 6y = -24 \lt 0</m>). Then <m>x = 4</m> and <m>xy^2 = 256</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-goat-pen-divided">
                <statement><p>Achieng has <m>48\,\text{m}</m> of fencing to make a rectangular goat pen divided into two equal parts by a fence parallel to one side. This uses three lengths <m>x</m> m and two lengths <m>y</m> m. Find the greatest total area.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>96\,\text{m}^2</m>, with <m>x = 8</m> and <m>y = 12</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>3x + 2y = 48</m>, so <m>y = 24 - 1.5x</m> and <m>A = xy = 24x - 1.5x^2</m>. <m>\dfrac{dA}{dx} = 24 - 3x = 0</m> at <m>x = 8</m>; <m>y = 12</m>; <m>A = 96</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-tray-from-a-rectangle">
                <statement><p>An open box is made from a sheet of card <m>21\,\text{cm}</m> by <m>16\,\text{cm}</m> by cutting squares of side <m>x</m> cm from the corners. Find the dimensions of the box with the greatest volume.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>15\,\text{cm}</m> by <m>10\,\text{cm}</m> by <m>3\,\text{cm}</m>; volume <m>450\,\text{cm}^3</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>V = x(21 - 2x)(16 - 2x) = 4x^3 - 74x^2 + 336x</m> for <m>0 \lt x \lt 8</m>. <m>\dfrac{dV}{dx} = 12x^2 - 148x + 336 = 4(3x - 28)(x - 3)</m>, zero at <m>x = 3</m> or <m>x = \dfrac{28}{3}</m>; but <m>\dfrac{28}{3} \gt 8</m> is outside the domain. <m>\dfrac{d^2V}{dx^2} = 24x - 148 = -76</m> at <m>x = 3</m>: a maximum.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-bus-fare">
                <statement><p>A bus company charges Ksh <m>p</m> for a ticket from Nairobi to Nakuru, and carries <m>1600 - 2p</m> passengers a day. Find the fare that gives the greatest daily takings, and the takings at that fare.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>Ksh <m>400</m>; <m>800</m> passengers; Ksh <m>320\,000</m> a day</p></answer>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-stool-workshop-profit">
                <statement><p>A jua kali workshop in Kamukunji makes <m>x</m> stools a week. Its weekly profit, <m>P</m> hundred shillings, is modelled by <m>P = -x^3 + 18x^2 - 60x - 40</m> for <m>0 \le x \le 12</m>. Find and classify the stationary points. How many stools give the greatest profit?</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>(2, -96)</m> minimum point; <m>(10, 160)</m> maximum point. <m>P(0) = -40</m> and <m>P(12) = 104</m>, so <m>10</m> stools give the greatest profit, Ksh <m>16\,000</m> a week.</p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>\dfrac{dP}{dx} = -3x^2 + 36x - 60 = -3(x - 2)(x - 10)</m>. <m>\dfrac{d^2P}{dx^2} = -6x + 36</m> is <m>24</m> at <m>x = 2</m> and <m>-24</m> at <m>x = 10</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-box-side-from-the-best-cut">
                <statement><p>The best cut for an open box made from a square sheet of side <m>s</m> cm is <m>x = 5\,\text{cm}</m>. Find <m>s</m>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>s = 30</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>V = x(s - 2x)^2</m> gives <m>\dfrac{dV}{dx} = (s - 2x)(s - 6x)</m>, so the best cut is <m>x = \dfrac{s}{6}</m>, and <m>\dfrac{s}{6} = 5</m> gives <m>s = 30</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-square-is-best">
                <title>Challenge: The Square Is Best</title>
                <statement><p>Show that of all rectangles with perimeter <m>4k</m>, the square has the greatest area.</p></statement>
                <hint><p>If one side is <m>x</m>, the other is <m>2k - x</m>.</p></hint>
                <answer><p><m>A = x(2k - x)</m>; <m>\dfrac{dA}{dx} = 2k - 2x = 0</m> at <m>x = k</m>, and <m>\dfrac{d^2A}{dx^2} = -2 \lt 0</m>. Both sides are then <m>k</m>: a square.</p></answer>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-maxima-and-minima-in-context">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Provide a model-building frame: diagram, constraint, quantity in one variable, domain. Work through the activity and the first two examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-greatest-product"/>, <xref ref="ex-goat-pen-divided"/> and <xref ref="ex-bus-fare"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-greatest-product"/> to <xref ref="ex-box-side-from-the-best-cut"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-square-is-best"/>. Extension (an investigation with no single answer): a box with a lid is made from a 24 cm square by cutting squares from two corners only and folding the flap over as a lid. Is its best volume larger or smaller than <m>1024\,\text{cm}^3</m>?</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 3.2 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-greatest-and-least-values">
        <title>Greatest and Least Values on an Interval</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> on a closed interval the greatest or least value may be at an end, and stationary points outside the interval do not count.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> compare the values at the stationary points and at the ends of an interval.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply (Extension):</alert> find the least material for a tank or a box, using negative powers.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners find that the highest point of a graph on an interval need not be a maximum point.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Recall part (e) of <xref ref="act-walking-the-ngong-hills-path"/>: the path was highest at its end. Ask: <q>Which values must we compare?</q> Let groups compare their lists of candidates before calculating.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-peak-or-edge">
            <title>Peak or Edge?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Let <m>f(x) = x^3 - 12x^2 + 36x + 10</m>.</p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Find and classify the stationary points of <m>y = f(x)</m>.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>f'(x) = 3x^2 - 24x + 36 = 3(x - 2)(x - 6)</m>: <m>(2, 42)</m> maximum point, <m>(6, 10)</m> minimum point.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Find <m>f(1)</m> and <m>f(9)</m>.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>35</m> and <m>91</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Find the greatest and least values of <m>f(x)</m> for <m>1 \le x \le 9</m>.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Greatest <m>91</m> at <m>x = 9</m> (an end); least <m>10</m> at <m>x = 6</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>What are the greatest and least values for <m>1 \le x \le 5</m>?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>f(5) = 15</m>. The point at <m>x = 6</m> is now outside the interval. Greatest <m>42</m> at <m>x = 2</m>; least <m>15</m> at <m>x = 5</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To set out the candidates for the greatest and least values on an interval.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> in part (c) the greatest value is at an end; in part (d) a stationary point drops out.</p></li>
                    <li><p>List the candidates (stationary points inside the interval, and both ends), then compare.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-greatest-and-least-values">
            <title>Greatest and Least Values</title>
            <p>
                The <term>greatest value</term> of <m>f(x)</m> on an interval <m>a \le x \le b</m> is the largest value it takes there; the <term>least value</term> is the smallest. Each occurs either at a stationary point inside the interval or at one of the ends, <m>x = a</m> or <m>x = b</m>. So work out <m>f</m> at each of these candidates and compare. Stationary points outside the interval are ignored.
            </p>
            <p>
                The best value of a quantity is at a stationary point or at an end of its domain.
            </p>
        </insight>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-checking-the-ends">
            <title>Checking the Ends</title>
            <statement><p>Find the greatest and least values of <m>f(x) = 2x^3 - 3x^2 - 12x + 5</m> for <m>-2 \le x \le 4</m>.</p></statement>
            <solution>
                <p><m>f'(x) = 6x^2 - 6x - 12 = 6(x + 1)(x - 2)</m>, so the stationary points are at <m>x = -1</m> and <m>x = 2</m>, both inside the interval.</p>
                <table xml:id="table-candidates-checking-the-ends">
                    <title>Candidates for the Greatest and Least Values</title>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell><m>x</m></cell><cell><m>-2</m> (end)</cell><cell><m>-1</m> (maximum point)</cell><cell><m>2</m> (minimum point)</cell><cell><m>4</m> (end)</cell></row>
                        <row><cell><m>f(x)</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>12</m></cell><cell><m>-15</m></cell><cell><m>37</m></cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <p>The greatest value is <m>37</m>, at the end <m>x = 4</m>. The least value is <m>-15</m>, at the minimum point <m>x = 2</m>.</p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-greatest-least-on-interval">
                    <caption><m>y = 2x^3 - 3x^2 - 12x + 5</m> for <m>-2 \le x \le 4</m>.</caption>
                    <image width="80%">
                        <shortdescription>Endpoints (-2, 1) and (4, 37), maximum point (-1, 12), minimum point (2, -15); the greatest value is at the right end.</shortdescription>
                        <description><p>Graph on a grid, x from -2 to 4, y from -20 to 40. The curve starts at (-2, 1), rises to the maximum point (-1, 12), falls to the minimum point (2, -15) and rises steeply to (4, 37). All four points are marked.</p></description>
                        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[clip=false, width=11cm, height=8cm, axis lines=middle, xmin=-2.5, xmax=4.5, ymin=-20, ymax=42, xtick={-2,...,4}, ytick={-20,-10,...,40}, grid=both, major grid style={gray!40}, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}]
\addplot[thick, domain=-2:4, samples=100] {2*x^3-3*x^2-12*x+5};
\addplot[only marks, mark=*] coordinates {(-2,1) (-1,12) (2,-15) (4,37)};
\node[anchor=east] at (axis cs:-2.05,3) {$(-2, 1)$};
\node[anchor=south] at (axis cs:-1,13) {$(-1, 12)$};
\node[anchor=north] at (axis cs:2,-16) {$(2, -15)$};
\node[anchor=east] at (axis cs:3.9,37) {$(4, 37)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-least-metal-for-a-water-tank">
            <title>The Least Metal for a Water Tank (Extension)</title>
            <statement><p>A closed cylindrical water tank for a school in Kitui must hold <m>16\pi\,\text{m}^3</m>. Find the radius and height that use the least metal.</p></statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Let the radius be <m>r</m> m and the height <m>h</m> m (<xref ref="fig-cylinder-tank"/>). The volume gives the constraint <m>\pi r^2 h = 16\pi</m>, so <m>h = \dfrac{16}{r^2}</m>.</p>
                <p>The metal is the surface area: two circles and the curved side.
                    <md>A = 2\pi r^2 + 2\pi r h = 2\pi r^2 + \frac{32\pi}{r} = 2\pi r^2 + 32\pi r^{-1}, \qquad r \gt 0</md></p>
                <p>Using the Grade 11 power rule for negative powers, <m>\dfrac{dA}{dr} = 4\pi r - 32\pi r^{-2} = 4\pi r - \dfrac{32\pi}{r^2}</m>. This is <m>0</m> when <m>r^3 = 8</m>, so <m>r = 2</m> and <m>h = \dfrac{16}{4} = 4</m>.</p>
                <p><m>\dfrac{d^2A}{dr^2} = 4\pi + \dfrac{64\pi}{r^3} \gt 0</m>, so this is a minimum. The tank should have radius <m>2\,\text{m}</m> and height <m>4\,\text{m}</m>, using <m>8\pi + 16\pi = 24\pi \approx 75.4\,\text{m}^2</m> of metal.</p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-cylinder-tank">
                    <caption>A closed cylindrical tank of radius <m>r</m> and height <m>h</m>.</caption>
                    <image width="35%">
                        <shortdescription>A closed cylinder with radius r and height h labelled.</shortdescription>
                        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[thick] (0,0) ellipse (1.2 and 0.35);
\draw[thick] (-1.2,0) -- (-1.2,-2.6);
\draw[thick] (1.2,0) -- (1.2,-2.6);
\draw[thick] (-1.2,-2.6) arc (180:360:1.2 and 0.35);
\draw[dashed] (-1.2,-2.6) arc (180:0:1.2 and 0.35);
\draw (0,0) -- (1.2,0) node[midway, above] {$r$};
\draw[&lt;-&gt;] (1.6,0) -- (1.6,-2.6) node[midway, right] {$h$};
\end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-workshop-at-full-capacity">
            <title>A Workshop at Full Capacity</title>
            <statement><p>The Kamukunji workshop of <xref ref="ex-stool-workshop-profit"/> can make at most <m>8</m> stools a week, so its profit <m>P = -x^3 + 18x^2 - 60x - 40</m> (hundred shillings) applies for <m>0 \le x \le 8</m>. How many stools give the greatest profit?</p></statement>
            <solution>
                <p><m>\dfrac{dP}{dx} = -3(x - 2)(x - 10)</m>. The stationary point at <m>x = 2</m> is inside the interval (<m>P = -96</m>, a minimum point); the one at <m>x = 10</m> is outside it and is ignored.</p>
                <p>The candidates are <m>P(0) = -40</m>, <m>P(2) = -96</m> and <m>P(8) = -512 + 1152 - 480 - 40 = 120</m>. The greatest profit is <m>120</m> hundred shillings, Ksh <m>12\,000</m> a week, at <m>x = 8</m>: at full capacity, an end of the interval.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type whole numbers or fractions, for example <c>-3</c> or <c>5/2</c>. Choose the step from the list.</p>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-greatest-and-least-values-on-an-interval">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-greatest-and-least-values-on-an-interval" source="stack/top/4-calculus/17-differentiation-ii/03-application-of-differentiation/Greatest-and-least-values-on-an-interval.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-finding-the-mistake-in-a-maximum-volume-calculation">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-finding-the-mistake-in-a-maximum-volume-calculation" source="stack/top/4-calculus/17-differentiation-ii/03-application-of-differentiation/Finding-the-mistake-in-a-maximum-volume-calculation.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercises xml:id="exercises-greatest-and-least-values">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-greatest-least-cubic">
                <statement><p>Find the greatest and least values of <m>f(x) = x^3 - 6x^2 - 15x + 4</m> for <m>-2 \le x \le 9</m>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>Greatest <m>112</m> (at <m>x = 9</m>); least <m>-96</m> (at <m>x = 5</m>)</p></answer>
                <solution><p>Stationary points at <m>x = -1</m> and <m>x = 5</m>. The candidates <m>f(-2), f(-1), f(5), f(9)</m> are <m>2, 12, -96, 112</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-greatest-least-twelve-x">
                <statement><p>Find the greatest and least values of <m>f(x) = 12x - x^3</m> for <m>0 \le x \le 3</m>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>Greatest <m>16</m> (at <m>x = 2</m>); least <m>0</m> (at <m>x = 0</m>)</p></answer>
                <solution><p>The stationary point at <m>x = -2</m> is outside the interval. <m>f(0) = 0</m>, <m>f(2) = 16</m>, <m>f(3) = 9</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-least-card-for-a-box">
                <title>Extension: The Least Card for a Box</title>
                <statement><p>An open box with a square base must hold <m>500\,\text{cm}^3</m>. Find the dimensions that use the least card, and the area of card used.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>Base <m>10\,\text{cm}</m> by <m>10\,\text{cm}</m>, height <m>5\,\text{cm}</m>; <m>300\,\text{cm}^2</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>With base <m>x</m> cm and height <m>h</m> cm, <m>x^2 h = 500</m>, so <m>A = x^2 + 4xh = x^2 + \dfrac{2000}{x}</m>. <m>\dfrac{dA}{dx} = 2x - \dfrac{2000}{x^2} = 0</m> gives <m>x^3 = 1000</m>, <m>x = 10</m>, <m>h = 5</m>, and <m>\dfrac{d^2A}{dx^2} = 2 + \dfrac{4000}{x^3} \gt 0</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-milk-bucket">
                <title>Extension: A Milk Bucket</title>
                <statement><p>An open cylindrical milk bucket must hold <m>1000\pi\,\text{cm}^3</m>. Find the radius and height that use the least metal.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>r = 10\,\text{cm}</m>, <m>h = 10\,\text{cm}</m>; <m>300\pi \approx 942.5\,\text{cm}^2</m> of metal</p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>\pi r^2 h = 1000\pi</m> gives <m>h = \dfrac{1000}{r^2}</m>, so <m>A = \pi r^2 + 2\pi r h = \pi r^2 + \dfrac{2000\pi}{r}</m>. <m>\dfrac{dA}{dr} = 2\pi r - \dfrac{2000\pi}{r^2} = 0</m> at <m>r^3 = 1000</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-medicine-concentration">
                <statement><p>The concentration of a medicine in a patient's blood, <m>C</m> mg per litre, <m>t</m> hours after a dose, is modelled by <m>C = 6t^2 - t^3</m>. Find the greatest concentration (a) in the first <m>6</m> hours; (b) in the first <m>3</m> hours.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>(a) <m>32</m> mg/L at <m>t = 4</m>; (b) <m>27</m> mg/L at <m>t = 3</m>, an end of the interval</p></answer>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-find-k-least-value">
                <statement><p>The least value of <m>f(x) = x^3 - 12x + k</m> on <m>0 \le x \le 3</m> is <m>3</m>. Find <m>k</m>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>k = 19</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>The candidates are <m>f(0) = k</m>, <m>f(2) = k - 16</m> and <m>f(3) = k - 9</m>. The least is <m>k - 16 = 3</m>, so <m>k = 19</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-closed-cylinder-h-equals-2r">
                <title>Challenge (Extension): Height Equals Diameter</title>
                <statement><p>Show that a closed cylinder of fixed volume <m>V</m> uses the least metal when <m>h = 2r</m>.</p></statement>
                <hint><p>Write the surface area in terms of <m>r</m> only.</p></hint>
                <answer><p><m>A = 2\pi r^2 + \dfrac{2V}{r}</m>; <m>\dfrac{dA}{dr} = 4\pi r - \dfrac{2V}{r^2} = 0</m> gives <m>V = 2\pi r^3</m>, so <m>h = \dfrac{V}{\pi r^2} = 2r</m>.</p></answer>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-greatest-and-least-values">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Provide a candidates table (<m>x</m>, <m>f(x)</m>, stationary point or end). Work through the activity and <xref ref="example-checking-the-ends"/>. Assign <xref ref="ex-greatest-least-cubic"/> and <xref ref="ex-greatest-least-twelve-x"/>. Leave out the Extension items.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-greatest-least-cubic"/> to <xref ref="ex-find-k-least-value"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-closed-cylinder-h-equals-2r"/>. Extension: does the open bucket of <xref ref="ex-milk-bucket"/> have <m>h = r</m> for every volume? (Answer: yes. <m>A = \pi r^2 + \dfrac{2V}{r}</m> is least when <m>V = \pi r^3</m>, so <m>h = \dfrac{V}{\pi r^2} = r</m>.)</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 3.3 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-kinematics-distance-and-greatest-velocity">
        <title>Kinematics: Distance, Speed and Greatest Velocity</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> the difference between displacement and distance travelled; speed; when the velocity is greatest; when a particle is speeding up or slowing down.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> find the times a particle is at rest, its positions then, the distance travelled and the greatest velocity.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> describe a motion in words.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners find that the distance a drone flies is not the same as its final displacement.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Act out the motion: one learner walks along a tape line on the floor, east positive, following the table in part (a). Before calculating part (d), groups argue whether the answer is <m>36\,\text{m}</m> or <m>44\,\text{m}</m> and listen to each other's reasons (critical thinking).</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-drone-over-the-tea">
            <title>A Drone over the Tea</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A drone sprays a row of tea bushes on an estate in Kericho. It flies along the row, and its displacement east of a post, <m>s</m> m, <m>t</m> seconds after it passes the post, is <m>s = \frac{1}{8}t^3 - \frac{9}{4}t^2 + 12t</m> for <m>0 \le t \le 12</m>.</p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Find the velocity <m>v = \dfrac{ds}{dt}</m>, and complete a table of <m>s</m> and <m>v</m> for <m>t = 0, 2, 4, \ldots, 12</m>.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>v = \frac{3}{8}t^2 - \frac{9}{2}t + 12</m>. <m>s = 0, 16, 20, 18, 16, 20, 36</m>; <m>v = 12, 4.5, 0, -1.5, 0, 4.5, 12</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>When is the drone instantaneously at rest?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>v = \frac{3}{8}(t - 4)(t - 8) = 0</m> at <m>t = 4</m> and <m>t = 8</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Where is the drone at <m>t = 12</m>?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>36\,\text{m}</m> east of the post.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>How far has it flown in the <m>12</m> seconds?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>East <m>20\,\text{m}</m> (to <m>s = 20</m>), back west <m>4\,\text{m}</m> (to <m>s = 16</m>), then east <m>20\,\text{m}</m> (to <m>s = 36</m>): <m>20 + 4 + 20 = 44\,\text{m}</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Find the acceleration <m>a = \dfrac{dv}{dt}</m>. When is <m>a = 0</m>, and what is <m>v</m> then?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>a = \frac{3}{4}t - \frac{9}{2} = 0</m> at <m>t = 6</m>, where <m>v = -1.5</m>: the drone is flying west at its fastest, with speed <m>1.5\,\text{m/s}</m>. (The acceleration stays between <m>-4.5</m> and <m>4.5\,\text{m/s}^2</m>.)</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To define displacement, distance travelled and speed, and to set out the rules for distance, greatest velocity and speeding up.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> part (d) splits the motion into stages between the rest times.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Speed is the size of the velocity, <m>|v|</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The greatest or least velocity is where <m>a = 0</m>, or at an end of the time interval.</p></li>
                    <li><p>When <m>v</m> and <m>a</m> have the same sign the particle speeds up.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-displacement-distance-speed">
            <title>Displacement, Distance and Speed</title>
            <p>
                As in Grade 11, a particle moves along a straight line; <m>s</m> metres is its <term>displacement</term> from a fixed point <m>O</m> at time <m>t</m> seconds (<m>t \ge 0</m>), <m>v = \dfrac{ds}{dt}</m> is its velocity in <m>\text{m/s}</m> and <m>a = \dfrac{dv}{dt} = \dfrac{d^2s}{dt^2}</m> its acceleration in <m>\text{m/s}^2</m>. When <m>v = 0</m> the particle is <term>instantaneously at rest</term>; it may change direction there.
            </p>
            <p>
                Displacement is where you are; <term>distance travelled</term> is how far you went. To find the distance travelled, split the motion at the times when the particle is at rest, and add the distances moved in each stage:
                <md>\text{distance} = |s(t_1) - s(0)| + |s(t_2) - s(t_1)| + \cdots</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                The <term>speed</term> is the size of the velocity, <m>|v|</m>. The velocity is greatest or least where <m>a = \dfrac{dv}{dt} = 0</m>, or at an end of the time interval. The particle is <term>speeding up</term> when <m>v</m> and <m>a</m> have the same sign (pulling the same way), and <term>slowing down</term> when their signs differ.
            </p>
        </insight>

        <warning xml:id="warning-displacement-not-distance">
            <title>Displacement Is Not Distance</title>
            <p>
                After <m>12</m> seconds the drone is <m>36\,\text{m}</m> from the post, but it has flown <m>44\,\text{m}</m>, because it turned back between <m>t = 4</m> and <m>t = 8</m>. Whenever a particle is at rest during the motion, check whether it changes direction there before you find the distance.
            </p>
        </warning>

        <figure xml:id="fig-drone-s-and-v">
            <caption>The drone's displacement (top) and velocity (bottom) against time.</caption>
            <image width="80%">
                <shortdescription>Displacement rises to 20 m at t = 4, falls to 16 m at t = 8, rises to 36 m at t = 12. Velocity is 0 at t = 4 and t = 8 and least, -1.5 m/s, at t = 6.</shortdescription>
                <description><p>Two graphs on the same time axis from 0 to 12 seconds, with dashed vertical lines at t = 4 and t = 8. Top: s against t through (0, 0), (4, 20), (8, 16), (12, 36), with the points (4, 20), (8, 16) and (12, 36) marked. Bottom: v against t, a U-shaped curve from 12 at t = 0 down to its least value -1.5 at t = 6 and back to 12 at t = 12, crossing zero at t = 4 and t = 8.</p></description>
                <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[clip=false, name=top, width=11cm, height=5.5cm, xmin=0, xmax=12.6, ymin=0, ymax=40, xtick={0,2,...,12}, ytick={0,10,...,40}, grid=both, major grid style={gray!40}, ylabel={$s$ (m)}, xticklabels={}]
\addplot[thick, domain=0:12, samples=100] {(x^3-18*x^2+96*x)/8};
\addplot[only marks, mark=*] coordinates {(4,20) (8,16) (12,36)};
\addplot[dashed] coordinates {(4,0) (4,40)}; \addplot[dashed] coordinates {(8,0) (8,40)};
\node[anchor=south] at (axis cs:4,20.5) {$(4, 20)$};
\node[anchor=north] at (axis cs:8,15.5) {$(8, 16)$};
\node[anchor=east] at (axis cs:11.9,36) {$(12, 36)$};
\end{axis}
\begin{axis}[clip=false, at={(top.south)}, anchor=north, yshift=-0.4cm, width=11cm, height=5.5cm, xmin=0, xmax=12.6, ymin=-3, ymax=13, xtick={0,2,...,12}, ytick={-2,0,...,12}, grid=both, major grid style={gray!40}, xlabel={$t$ (s)}, ylabel={$v$ (m/s)}]
\addplot[thick, domain=0:12, samples=100] {(3*x^2-36*x+96)/8};
\addplot[only marks, mark=*] coordinates {(6,-1.5)};
\addplot[dashed] coordinates {(4,-3) (4,13)}; \addplot[dashed] coordinates {(8,-3) (8,13)};
\node[anchor=south] at (axis cs:6,-1.3) {$(6, -1.5)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-distance-travelled">
            <title>Distance Travelled</title>
            <statement><p>A particle moves so that <m>s = t^3 - 12t^2 + 36t</m> metres after <m>t</m> seconds. Find its displacement at <m>t = 8</m> and the distance it travels in the first <m>8</m> seconds.</p></statement>
            <solution>
                <p><m>v = 3t^2 - 24t + 36 = 3(t - 2)(t - 6)</m>, so the particle is at rest at <m>t = 2</m> and <m>t = 6</m>.</p>
                <p><m>s(0) = 0</m>, <m>s(2) = 8 - 48 + 72 = 32</m>, <m>s(6) = 216 - 432 + 216 = 0</m>, <m>s(8) = 512 - 768 + 288 = 32</m>.</p>
                <p>The displacement at <m>t = 8</m> is <m>32\,\text{m}</m>. The distance is <m>|32 - 0| + |0 - 32| + |32 - 0| = 32 + 32 + 32 = 96\,\text{m}</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-sprinters-greatest-velocity">
            <title>A Sprinter's Greatest Velocity</title>
            <statement><p>During the first <m>10</m> seconds of a race at Kasarani, a sprinter's velocity is modelled by <m>v = 2.4t - 0.12t^2\,\text{m/s}</m>. Find the sprinter's greatest velocity, and the acceleration at the start.</p></statement>
            <solution>
                <p><m>a = \dfrac{dv}{dt} = 2.4 - 0.24t</m>, which is <m>0</m> at <m>t = 10</m>. Before that <m>a \gt 0</m>, so <m>v</m> increases up to <m>t = 10</m>: <m>v(10) = 24 - 12 = 12\,\text{m/s}</m> is the greatest velocity.</p>
                <p>At the start, <m>a(0) = 2.4\,\text{m/s}^2</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-speeding-up-or-slowing-down">
            <title>Speeding Up or Slowing Down?</title>
            <statement><p>A particle moves so that <m>s = 2t^3 - 15t^2 + 24t</m> metres after <m>t</m> seconds. Describe its motion at <m>t = 2</m>, <m>t = 3</m> and <m>t = 5</m>.</p></statement>
            <solution>
                <p><m>v = 6t^2 - 30t + 24 = 6(t - 1)(t - 4)</m> and <m>a = 12t - 30</m>.</p>
                <p>At <m>t = 2</m>: <m>v = -12</m>, <m>a = -6</m>. Same signs: the particle is moving in the negative direction and speeding up.</p>
                <p>At <m>t = 3</m>: <m>v = -12</m>, <m>a = 6</m>. Opposite signs: moving in the negative direction and slowing down.</p>
                <p>At <m>t = 5</m>: <m>v = 24</m>, <m>a = 30</m>. Same signs: moving in the positive direction and speeding up.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <paragraphs xml:id="para-where-differentiation-is-used">
            <title>Where Differentiation Is Used</title>
            <p>
                Every example in this chapter is a real kind of decision. Engineers find the least material for a part, as for the water tank in <xref ref="example-least-metal-for-a-water-tank"/>. Traders choose the price that gives the greatest profit. Doctors plan doses so that the concentration of a medicine reaches its greatest value safely. Coaches study when an athlete reaches top speed. In each case the answer comes from where a derivative is zero.
            </p>
            <p>
                Think of one decision in your home or community that could be improved by finding a greatest or least value. What quantity would you differentiate?
            </p>
            <p>
                In the next chapter you will reverse this: from velocity back to displacement.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Curriculum Links</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Key Inquiry Question</title><p>How can differentiation help us find the best possible value of a quantity, and describe how things move? Revisit it in the reflection.</p></li>
                <li><title>Core Competencies</title><p>Critical thinking, in deciding between displacement and distance in the drone activity, and in the find-the-mistake questions; self-efficacy, in predicting, building and testing the best box.</p></li>
                <li><title>Values</title><p>Respect, in listening to other groups' predictions and reasons before judging them.</p></li>
                <li><title>Pertinent and Contemporary Issues</title><p>Online safety, in using approved graphing apps without sharing personal details or location.</p></li>
                <li><title>Learning Outcome</title><p>This lesson carries the outcome on appreciating the use of differentiation in real life.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type whole numbers or fractions, for example <c>-3</c> or <c>5/2</c>. For each time, choose the description from the list.</p>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-displacement-and-distance-travelled-by-a-particle">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-displacement-and-distance-travelled-by-a-particle" source="stack/top/4-calculus/17-differentiation-ii/03-application-of-differentiation/Displacement-and-distance-travelled-by-a-particle.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-greatest-velocity-of-a-particle">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-greatest-velocity-of-a-particle" source="stack/top/4-calculus/17-differentiation-ii/03-application-of-differentiation/Greatest-velocity-of-a-particle.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-speeding-up-or-slowing-down">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-speeding-up-or-slowing-down" source="stack/top/4-calculus/17-differentiation-ii/03-application-of-differentiation/Speeding-up-or-slowing-down.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercises xml:id="exercises-kinematics-distance-greatest-velocity">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-rest-times-and-positions">
                <statement><p>A particle moves so that <m>s = t^3 - 9t^2 + 15t</m> metres after <m>t</m> seconds. When is it at rest? Find its displacement and acceleration at those times.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>t = 1</m>: <m>s = 7\,\text{m}</m>, <m>a = -12\,\text{m/s}^2</m>. <m>t = 5</m>: <m>s = -25\,\text{m}</m>, <m>a = 12\,\text{m/s}^2</m>.</p></answer>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-distance-in-six-seconds">
                <statement><p>For the particle in <xref ref="ex-rest-times-and-positions"/>, find the displacement at <m>t = 6</m> and the distance travelled in the first <m>6</m> seconds.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>-18\,\text{m}</m>; <m>7 + 32 + 7 = 46\,\text{m}</m></p></answer>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-cyclist-greatest-velocity">
                <statement><p>A cyclist on the Eldoret to Iten road has velocity <m>v = 0.6t - 0.015t^2\,\text{m/s}</m> for <m>0 \le t \le 40</m>. Find the greatest velocity.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>6\,\text{m/s}</m>, at <m>t = 20</m></p></answer>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-speeding-or-slowing">
                <statement><p>A particle moves so that <m>s = t^3 - 6t^2 + 5</m> metres after <m>t</m> seconds. Is it speeding up or slowing down at <m>t = 1</m>, <m>t = 3</m> and <m>t = 5</m>?</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>t = 1</m>: <m>v = -9</m>, <m>a = -6</m>, speeding up. <m>t = 3</m>: <m>v = -9</m>, <m>a = 6</m>, slowing down. <m>t = 5</m>: <m>v = 15</m>, <m>a = 18</m>, speeding up.</p></answer>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-stone-from-a-building">
                <statement><p>A stone is thrown upwards from the top of a <m>25\,\text{m}</m> building in Nairobi. Its height above the ground after <m>t</m> seconds is <m>s = 25 + 20t - 5t^2</m> metres (using <m>g = 10\,\text{m/s}^2</m>). Find its greatest height, when it reaches the ground and its speed then, and the total distance it travels.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>Greatest height <m>45\,\text{m}</m> at <m>t = 2</m>; it reaches the ground at <m>t = 5</m> with speed <m>30\,\text{m/s}</m>; total distance <m>20 + 45 = 65\,\text{m}</m>.</p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>v = 20 - 10t = 0</m> at <m>t = 2</m>, <m>s(2) = 45</m>. <m>s = 0</m>: <m>5t^2 - 20t - 25 = 5(t - 5)(t + 1) = 0</m>, so <m>t = 5</m>, and <m>v(5) = -30</m>. It rises <m>20\,\text{m}</m> and then falls <m>45\,\text{m}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-find-p-and-q-rest-times">
                <statement><p>A particle moves so that <m>s = t^3 + pt^2 + qt</m> metres after <m>t</m> seconds. It is at rest at <m>t = 1</m> and <m>t = 7</m>. Find <m>p</m> and <m>q</m>, and the acceleration at <m>t = 4</m>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>p = -12</m>, <m>q = 21</m>; <m>a(4) = 0</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>v = 3t^2 + 2pt + q</m> must be <m>3(t - 1)(t - 7) = 3t^2 - 24t + 21</m>. Then <m>a = 6t - 24</m>, which is <m>0</m> at <m>t = 4</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-at-rest-once-never-turns">
                <title>Challenge: At Rest but Never Turning</title>
                <statement><p>Show that a particle with <m>s = t^3 - 3t^2 + 3t</m> is at rest once but never changes direction.</p></statement>
                <hint><p>Factorise <m>v</m>.</p></hint>
                <answer><p><m>v = 3t^2 - 6t + 3 = 3(t - 1)^2</m>. It is <m>0</m> only at <m>t = 1</m>, and never negative, so the particle always moves in the positive direction.</p></answer>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-kinematics-distance-greatest-velocity">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Provide a three-column card (<m>s</m>, <m>v = \dfrac{ds}{dt}</m>, <m>a = \dfrac{dv}{dt}</m>) and a stage table for distance. Work through the activity, <xref ref="example-distance-travelled"/> and <xref ref="example-speeding-up-or-slowing-down"/>. Assign <xref ref="ex-rest-times-and-positions"/>, <xref ref="ex-distance-in-six-seconds"/> and <xref ref="ex-speeding-or-slowing"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-rest-times-and-positions"/> to <xref ref="ex-find-p-and-q-rest-times"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-at-rest-once-never-turns"/>. Extension: find a motion <m>s = t^3 + bt^2 + ct</m> that is at rest at <m>t = 2</m> and <m>t = 5</m>. What is its least velocity? (Answer: <m>b = -\dfrac{21}{2}</m>, <m>c = 30</m>; least velocity <m>-6.75\,\text{m/s}</m> at <m>t = 3.5</m>.)</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>

